# SQL Validation Patterns

## Case-Insensitive Keyword Handling

The library automatically handles case-insensitive SQL keywords. Reference [CaseInsensitiveSnowflakeLexer.ts](mdc:src/CaseInsensitiveSnowflakeLexer.ts) for implementation details.

### Supported Patterns
- ✅ `select column1 from table1` (lowercase)
- ✅ `SELECT COLUMN1 FROM TABLE1` (uppercase)  
- ✅ `Select Column1 From Table1` (title case)
- ❌ `sElEcT cOlUmN1 fRoM tAbLe1` (mixed case)

### Implementation Guidelines
- Use Map-based lookups for fast keyword recognition
- Preserve identifier case (MyColumn, MyTable stay as-is)
- Reject mixed-case keywords that could cause parsing issues
- Cache normalized SQL for performance

## Fast-Path Validation

Reference [src/index.ts](mdc:src/index.ts) for fast-path validation implementation:

### Basic Checks
- Balanced parentheses and quotes
- Valid SQL statement starts (SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, etc.)
- Missing FROM clauses in SELECT statements
- Duplicate keywords detection
- Basic syntax structure validation

### Performance Optimization
- Skip expensive ANTLR4 parsing for simple queries
- Use regex patterns for quick syntax validation
- Cache parser instances for repeated use
- Implement LRU cache management

## Error Reporting

Use consistent error interfaces defined in [SnowflakeValidationVisitor.ts](mdc:src/SnowflakeValidationVisitor.ts):

```typescript
interface ValidationError {
  startLine: number;
  endLine: number;
  startColumn: number;
  endColumn: number;
  message: string;
  severity: 'error' | 'warning' | 'info';
  suggestions: string[];
}
```

## Custom Validation Rules

Extend [SnowflakeValidationVisitor.ts](mdc:src/SnowflakeValidationVisitor.ts) for custom validation:

```typescript
class CustomValidationVisitor extends SnowflakeValidationVisitor {
  visitTerminal(node: any): any[] {
    const errors = super.visitTerminal(node);
    // Add custom validation logic here
    return errors;
  }
}
```

## Performance Considerations

- Cache normalized SQL strings to avoid repeated processing
- Use batch processing for multiple SQL statements
- Implement debouncing for user input validation
- Monitor cache hit rates and memory usage
- Clear caches periodically in long-running applications
